---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {datetimeSidebar} from '../../../data/datetime-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Kronos Clock" sidebar={datetimeSidebar}
            description="Injectable, testable time source for production and tests">

    <h1>Kronos Clock</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        An injectable time source. System clock in production, fixed or advancing clock in tests.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem with "now"</h2>

    <p>Calling <code>Clock.System.now()</code> directly makes code hard to test. You can't control what "now" means, so
        you end up with flaky tests, <code>Thread.sleep()</code> hacks, or mocking libraries. Kronos solves this by
        making
        the clock an explicit dependency.</p>

    <Aside>If you've ever written a test that passes at 11:59 PM but fails at midnight, you know why this matters.
    </Aside>

    <h2>System clock</h2>

    <p>In production, use the system clock:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.datetime.Kronos

val kronos = Kronos.systemUtc

val now = kronos.instantNow()
val today = kronos.localDateNow(MpTimezone.UTC)
val currentTime = kronos.localDateTimeNow()
val zoned = kronos.zonedDateTimeNow(MpTimezone.of("Europe/Berlin"))`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Fixed clock</h2>

    <p>In tests, freeze time at a specific instant:</p>

    <Code code={`val frozen = Kronos.fixed(MpInstant.parse("2025-06-15T12:00:00Z"))

frozen.instantNow()  // always 2025-06-15T12:00:00Z
frozen.instantNow()  // still 2025-06-15T12:00:00Z

// Fix to "now" at construction time
val frozenNow = Kronos.fixedNow()`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Advancing clock</h2>

    <p><code>advanceBy</code> returns a new Kronos that runs ahead of (or behind) the one it wraps:</p>

    <Code code={`import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.hours
import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.days
import kotlin.time.Duration.Companion.minutes

val kronos = Kronos.fixed(MpInstant.parse("2025-06-15T12:00:00Z"))

val advanced = kronos.advanceBy(2.hours)
advanced.instantNow()  // 2025-06-15T14:00:00Z

val nextDay = advanced.advanceBy(1.days)
nextDay.instantNow()   // 2025-06-16T14:00:00Z`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>Chained advances, including repeated <code>{"mutable.mutate { advanceBy(..) }"}</code> steps, collapse into a
        single offset, so a clock advanced a million times reads as fast as one advanced once.</p>

    <p>When the offset is not known yet, pass a lambda. It is called once, the first time the clock is read, never
        while chaining. If it throws, it is called again on the next read.</p>

    <Code code={`// The offset lives in the database, which is not ready while the app boots
val kronos = Kronos.systemUtc.advanceBy { settings.loadClockOffset() }`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>Mutable clock</h2>

    <p>For tests and simulations that move time mid-execution. <code>mutate</code> replaces the clock with what the
        block returns, <code>set</code> replaces it outright:</p>

    <Code code={`val mutable = Kronos.fixed(MpInstant.parse("2025-01-01T00:00:00Z")).mutable()

mutable.mutate { advanceBy(1.hours) }
mutable.instantNow()  // 2025-01-01T01:00:00Z

mutable.set(Kronos.fixed(MpInstant.parse("2030-01-01T00:00:00Z")))
mutable.instantNow()  // 2030-01-01T00:00:00Z`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><code>advanceBy</code> on a mutable does not move it: it returns a new clock that follows the mutable.
        <code>set</code> and <code>mutate</code> throw <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> for a clock that reads
        the mutable itself, which would recurse forever on every read. The check sees through mutables and
        <code>advanceBy</code> layers only, not into <code>{"advanceBy { }"}</code> lambdas or custom clocks, and is
        not safe against two threads setting two mutables to read each other.</p>

    <Aside>„<code>{"mutable.mutate { mutable.advanceBy(1.hours) }"}</code> looks like it advances the clock. It asks the
        clock to read itself, forever. It used to end in a stack overflow on the first read, now it ends at the call
        site."
    </Aside>

    <h2>Injecting with Kontainer</h2>

    <p>Kronos works naturally with <a href="/ultra/kontainer/">Kontainer</a> for dependency injection:</p>

    <Code code={`// Production setup
val blueprint = kontainer {
    singleton(Kronos::class) { Kronos.systemUtc }
}

// Test setup — override with a fixed clock
val testBlueprint = kontainer {
    singleton(Kronos::class) {
        Kronos.fixed(MpInstant.parse("2025-06-15T12:00:00Z"))
    }
}

// Services just inject Kronos
class EventService(private val kronos: Kronos) {
    fun createEvent(name: String) = Event(
        name = name,
        createdAt = kronos.instantNow(),
    )
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <h2>KronosDescriptor</h2>

    <p><code>describe()</code> returns a serializable <code>KronosDescriptor</code>, and
        <code>descriptor.instantiate()</code> turns it back into a Kronos:</p>

    <Code code={`val descriptor = Kronos.systemUtc.advanceBy(10.minutes).advanceBy(1.hours).describe()
// KronosDescriptor.AdvancedBy(durationMs = 4_200_000, inner = KronosDescriptor.SystemClock)

val kronos = descriptor.instantiate()`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>A chain of advances describes as one <code>AdvancedBy</code>. Nested descriptors written by older versions
        still instantiate.</p>

    <Aside>„A fixed clock describes itself as the system clock, so it does not survive the round trip. Good enough
        for shifting a server's idea of now; not a way to persist a frozen test clock."
    </Aside>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4">
        <a href="/ultra/datetime/"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-white/5 text-gray-300 font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-white/10 transition-colors">
            &larr; Overview
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
